Short (tab)\" — I can create reactions on any node; targeting the tab instance works but the whole tabs block becomes clickable — acceptable? Better: inside component, Short tab is a frame/text child; on the instance, that child node exists — I can set reaction on instance.children[i]. Reactions on children of instances: allowed? Setting reactions on sublayers of an instance — I believe prototype reactions can be set on instance children (overrides). Hmm, actually reactions on instance sublayers might be restricted. Let me think... In Figma UI you CAN add interactions to nested elements of an instance. Yes, you can (interactions on instance children are supported). Plugin API: node.reactions setter — should work on instance children. I'll try; fallback: overlay a transparent rect over the Short tab area in the screen frame and attach the link.\n\nSafer approach: in each screen, add a transparent \"hotspot\" rectangle over the tab area for Short → 06. Hotspots are a normal prototyping pattern. I'll use hotspots where nested-instance targeting is risky: story avatars (Claire instance is an instance — attach reaction to the instance node itself, not its children — that's fine: reactions on an instance node are OK), tabs (Dating tab inside tab bar instance — attach to a hotspot rect over it OR to the tab bar child). I'll test setting reaction on instance child; if error, use hotspots. To minimize risk I'll just use hotspot rectangles everywhere for nested targets. Hmm, but clean prototype = interactions on real elements. Mixed approach: attach reactions to top-level nodes (buttons, avatars instances, text nodes I created directly in frame) directly; use hotspots only for nested-in-instance targets (tab items, Short tab, profile portrait tab? the whole tab bar instance could carry... no—different tabs different links).\n\nPlan for tab links: place transparent hotspot rects (named \"Hotspot · Dating tab\" etc.) in each screen over the tab positions. Tab bar component: 5 equal slots at fixed x positions: item width 393/5=78.6; centers at 39.3, 117.9, 196.5, 275.1, 353.7. Hotspot rect = 78×48 at x = i*78.6, y = tabBarY. predictable. OK.\n\nFor story avatars: the Claire avatar is a \"Story Avatar\" instance → attach reaction directly on that instance node. Direct instance reactions are fine (I'm fairly confident).\n\nFor menu rows (Setting etc.) no links needed. Show Profile row + close in drawer: those are nodes I create in the drawer frame directly (drawer built as frame, not instance) → attach directly.\n\nWait — should the drawer be a component instanced into 08? The brief: \"08_Menu is a drawer over 07_Profile\" as a frame; the drawer content itself could be a component \"Drawer\" instanced in 08_Menu frame over dimmed 07 backdrop. I'll make drawer a component \"Menu Drawer\" (with account block + logo + 8 rows) instanced in 08. Rows inside are instances of Menu Row component. Links: attach to drawer instance children (close button, Show Profile text) — nested again → hotspots in 08 frame over those positions, or build drawer directly in frame without component. Simplicity: build the drawer panel directly in the 08 frame (still composed of Menu Row instances + Icon Button instances). The \"reusable\" requirement is met by Menu Row etc. The drawer itself as a component adds little. Similarly sheet in 02 built directly in frame.\n\nHmm, brief says \"the sheet, the drawer ... as reusable components in your file\". OK — they explicitly list \"sign-in sheet, drawer and menu rows\" among components to build. Fine, I'll make:\n- \"Sign-in Sheet\" component (built once, instanced in 02)\n- \"Menu Drawer\" component (instanced in 08)\nBoth contain menu row / icon instances. Links: attach to instance children (close icon instance, Face ID button, Show Profile) — nested-in-instance reactions. If that fails, hotspots. I'll attempt nested and verify via screenshot + a test read.\n\nActually — I realize setting `reactions` on nested instance children IS supported in the plugin API (it's an override like any other). I'm fairly confident. I'll do it and verify.\n\n**Backdrop dimming**: 02 frame: build welcome backdrop (via shared helper code duplicated in that call), then overlay rect black 45% full 393×852, then sheet instance at bottom. 08 frame: build 07 backdrop via helper, overlay 40% black, drawer instance.\n\nTo reuse \"backdrop builder\" across calls, I'll define buildWelcomeBackdrop(parentFrame) inside the same call as frames 01+02 (one call builds both). And buildProfileBackdrop inside call for 07+08. \n\n**Status bar / home indicator instances**: instance from components 1:33 / 1:44 directly (they're components in this file). For dark screens (06 short video), recolor glyphs white: status bar instance children: text 9:41, signal rects, wifi ellipse, battery → override fills/strokes to white. Home indicator: rectangle fill white. On light screens leave black. Dynamic Island stays black always (it's black hardware). \n\n**06 top/bottom gradients**: gradient fills: GradientPaint linear with stops rgba(0,0,0,0.6)→transparent. gradientTransform needed: for vertical top gradient: gradientTransform [[0,1,0],[-1,0,1]]? The transform maps... I remember: for a top-to-bottom fade, gradientTransform = [[0, 1, 0], [-1, 0, 1]]. Let me just use that known matrix: vertical gradient (top→bottom): [[0,1,0],[-1,0,1]]. Horizontal (left→right): [[1,0,0],[0,1,0]]. I'll use these.\n\n**Like counts on 06**: right rail: heart icon white + \"123\" white 12 below, chat + \"89\", send, more. Bottom-left: avatar+name+Follow pill (white border pill, white text? \"Follow\" small pill plum bg white text), caption lines, music row with marquee dots? Keep text.\n\n**Now colors as RGB tuples** for code:\n- plum #4B164C = (0.2941, 0.0863, 0.2980)\n- orchid #DD88CF = (0.8667, 0.5333, 0.8118)\n- ink #1A1A1F = (0.1020, 0.1020, 0.1216)\n- ink2 #6B6570 = (0.4196, 0.3961, 0.4392)\n- ink3 #9B94A1 = (0.6078, 0.5804, 0.6314)\n- surface #F6F3F7 = (0.9647, 0.9529, 0.9686)\n- border #E7E1E9 = (0.9059, 0.8824, 0.9137)\n- like #FF4D6D = (1, 0.3020, 0.4275)\n- green #34C759 = (0.2039, 0.7804, 0.3490)\n- white (1,1,1), black (0,0,0)\n- tint orchid light #F8ECF6 = (0.9725, 0.9255, 0.9647)\n\n**Font loads**: Inter Regular, Medium, Semi Bold, Bold, Extra Bold.\n\nHelper functions (paste in every build call):\n```js\nconst F = { R:{family:\"Inter\",style:\"Regular\"}, M:{family:\"Inter\",style:\"Medium\"}, SB:{family:\"Inter\",style:\"Semi Bold\"}, B:{family:\"Inter\",style:\"Bold\"}, XB:{family:\"Inter\",style:\"Extra Bold\"} };\nawait Promise.all(Object.values(F).map(f=>figma.loadFontAsync(f)));\nconst C = { plum:{r:0.2941,g:0.0863,b:0.2980}, orchid:{r:0.8667,g:0.5333,b:0.8118}, ink:{r:0.102,g:0.102,b:0.1216}, ink2:{r:0.4196,g:0.3961,b:0.4392}, ink3:{r:0.6078,g:0.5804,b:0.6314}, surface:{r:0.9647,g:0.9529,b:0.9686}, border:{r:0.9059,g:0.8824,b:0.9137}, like:{r:1,g:0.302,b:0.4275}, green:{r:0.2039,g:0.7804,b:0.3490}, white:{r:1,g:1,b:1}, black:{r:0,g:0,b:0}, tint:{r:0.9725,g:0.9255,b:0.9647} };\nconst S=(c,o=1)=>[{type:\"SOLID\",color:c,opacity:o}];\nasync function N(id){return await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(id)}\nasync function comp(name){return (await figma.currentPage.findOne(n=>n.type===\"COMPONENT\"&&n.name===name))}\nfunction rect(p,name,w,h,x,y,fills,r=0){const rc=figma.createRectangle();rc.resize(w,h);rc.x=x;rc.y=y;if(r)rc.cornerRadius=r;rc.fills=fills;rc.name=name;p.appendChild(rc);return rc}\nfunction txt(p,characters,font,size,color,x,y,opts={}){const t=figma.createText();t.fontName=font;t.fontSize=size;t.characters=characters;t.fills=S(color,opts.o??1);p.appendChild(t);t.x=x;t.y=y;if(opts.w){t.textAutoResize=opts.h?\"NONE\":\"HEIGHT\";t.resize(opts.w,t.height);}if(opts.align)t.textAlignHorizontal=opts.align;if(opts.lh)t.lineHeight={value:opts.lh,unit:\"PIXELS\"};if(opts.ls)t.letterSpacing={value:opts.ls,unit:\"PERCENT\"};t.name=opts.name||characters.slice(0,30);return t}\n```\netc.\n\nNote: figma.createText() then set fontName before characters — must load font first (done). \n\nFrame creation: figma.createFrame(); f.resize(393,852); f.name=\"01_Welcome\"; f.fills=...; page.appendChild(f); f.x=..., f.y=...\n\nSections: figma.createSection? Page sections: figma.createSection() exists. I'll create one section \"Components\" to hold all components at x=-1400. Components placed inside section at coords. Or skip section (components can just sit on page left side). Section is nice for organization. figma.createSection() — supported. section.resizeWithoutConstraints(w,h). I'll add components into section (section.appendChild(comp)). Hmm sections can't have children directly? Sections CAN contain nodes (appendChild works). OK try; fallback to page-level placement.\n\nLet me also think about the workspace layout coordinates:\n- Components section: x=-1500, y=0, size 1350×2600 (grid of components 2 cols).\n- Frames: 01 (0,0), 02 (493,0), 03 (986,0); 04 (0,952), 05 (493,952), 06 (986,952); 07 (0,1904), 08 (493,1904).\n\n**Prototype reactions syntax**:\n```js\nnode.reactions = [{ action: { type:\"NODE\", destinationId:\"9:99\", navigation:\"NAVIGATE\", transition:{ type:\"MOVE_IN\", direction:\"BOTTOM\", duration:0.3, easing:{type:\"EASE_OUT\"} } }, trigger:{ type:\"ON_TAP\" } }];\n```\nHmm transition easing shape: { type: \"EASE_OUT\" }? Plugin API easing: { type: \"EASE_IN_AND_OUT\" | \"EASE_IN\" | \"EASE_OUT\" | \"LINEAR\" | \"GENTLE\" | \"QUICK\" | \"BOUNCY\" | \"CUSTOM_CUBIC_BEZIER\", ... }. I'll use { type: \"EASE_OUT\" }? Hmm, \"GENTLE\"/\"QUICK\" are figma's curves. Safe: { type: \"EASE_IN_AND_OUT\" }? For duration-based: transition: { type:\"MOVE_IN\", direction:\"BOTTOM\", duration:0.3, easing:{type:\"EASE_OUT\"} }? Hmm actually I recall easing objects like {type:\"GENTLE\"} valid. I'll use EASE_OUT; if it errors, adjust.\n\nSimplest robust: transition: {type:\"INSTANT\"}? Smart animate: {type:\"SMART_ANIMATE\", duration:0.3, easing:{...}}. I'll try with try/catch